// =====================================================================
// RULE:      Legacy authentication sign-in
// SEVERITY:  Medium (High if successful against a privileged account)
// ATT&CK:    Initial Access — T1078.004 (Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts)
//            Credential Access — T1110 (Brute Force), for the volume variant
// CONTROLS:  NIST 800-53 IA-2(1), AC-7, AU-6 · NERC CIP-005-7 R2 · CIP-007-6 R4
// SOURCE:    SigninLogs  (Entra ID connector)
// SCHEDULE:  run every 1h, look back 1h
// ENTITIES:  Account = UserPrincipalName · IP = IPAddress
//
// ===== NOT ENABLED IN THIS LAB, AND HERE IS WHY =====
// This rule needs the Entra ID sign-in logs connector, which is one of the
// high-volume ones and therefore one of the expensive ones. The lab runs on
// the Azure Activity connector only, deliberately, to keep ingestion near zero.
//
// The rule is included complete so the detection set is coherent, and marked
// as not-run so nothing here is overclaimed. In an interview: "I wrote it, I
// didn't run it, because enabling that connector in a personal subscription
// costs more than the lab is worth — here is the query and here is what it
// would catch." That answer is stronger than quietly presenting five rules as
// though all five were live.
//
// WHY THIS RULE MATTERS
// Legacy authentication protocols — POP, IMAP, SMTP AUTH, older Exchange and
// ActiveSync clients — cannot perform multi-factor authentication. They do not
// bypass MFA by exploit; they predate it. So every legacy-auth path is a
// username-and-password door into a tenant that otherwise requires MFA, and it
// is where password spraying goes first.
//
// The Conditional Access design in Lab C blocks legacy authentication. This is
// the detection for the case where that policy is missing, excluded, or the
// account is in a break-glass exclusion group.
// =====================================================================

SigninLogs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)
| where ClientAppUsed in (
      "Exchange ActiveSync",
      "IMAP4",
      "POP3",
      "SMTP",
      "MAPI Over HTTP",
      "Exchange Web Services",
      "Exchange Online PowerShell",
      "Authenticated SMTP",
      "Other clients"
  )
| extend Outcome = iff(ResultType == "0", "Success", "Failure")
| project
    TimeGenerated,
    UserPrincipalName,
    ClientAppUsed,
    Outcome,
    ResultType,
    ResultDescription,
    IPAddress,
    Location = tostring(LocationDetails.countryOrRegion),
    AppDisplayName
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE VARIANT THAT ACTUALLY CATCHES AN ATTACK
// Password spraying: many accounts, few attempts each, one source.
// Low per-account failure counts are exactly how a spray evades lockout
// thresholds, so counting distinct ACCOUNTS per IP beats counting failures
// per account.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// SigninLogs
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)
// | where ClientAppUsed in ("IMAP4","POP3","SMTP","Other clients","Authenticated SMTP")
// | where ResultType != "0"
// | summarize
//     DistinctAccounts = dcount(UserPrincipalName),
//     Attempts         = count(),
//     Accounts         = make_set(UserPrincipalName, 25),
//     FirstSeen        = min(TimeGenerated),
//     LastSeen         = max(TimeGenerated)
//   by IPAddress
// | where DistinctAccounts >= 5
// | extend AttemptsPerAccount = round(todouble(Attempts) / DistinctAccounts, 1)
// | order by DistinctAccounts desc
//
// AttemptsPerAccount near 1 is the spray signature. A high value is a single
// compromised or misconfigured client, which is a different incident with a
// different response.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// IF THIS FIRES WITH Outcome == "Success"
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//   1. Treat the account as compromised. A successful legacy-auth sign-in in a
//      tenant that requires MFA means MFA was not applied to that session.
//   2. Revoke sessions, reset credentials, review mailbox forwarding rules —
//      a forwarding rule is the usual persistence step after mailbox access and
//      it survives the password reset.
//   3. Then fix the cause: block legacy authentication tenant-wide and review
//      why an exclusion existed.
